## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design resilient, scalable OTCA pipelines that cope with edge intermittency and cross-region data flight. It emphasizes practical consistency, privacy, and schema strategy under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge buffering and bounded memory
- Per-tenant privacy masking
- Deduplication and idempotent writes
- Avro schema evolution (backward/forward compat)
- Region-aware routing and cross-region aggregation
- Backpressure and QoS controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe check (edge/local)
function isDuplicate(event, seen) {
  const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.event_id}`;
  const last = seen.get(key) || -Infinity;
  if (event.ts <= last) return true;
  seen.set(key, event.ts);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region reconciliation latency?
- What changes if edge buffers exceed memory under bursty traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design systems that balance regulatory compliance (GDPR, CCPA) with operational resilience. Instant consent revocation is critical for multi-tenant SaaS platforms where data processing must stop immediately upon user request. The OTCA framework ensures observability, transformation, compliance, and auditing are built into the data path rather than bolted on afterward.

## Key Concepts

- **Edge policy evaluation and dynamic consent revocation**: Decoupling policy decisions from the control plane enables sub-second enforcement without round-trip latency.
- **Per-tenant residency enforcement**: Data sovereignty requirements mandate that telemetry remain within geographic boundaries based on tenant configuration.
- **On-the-fly redaction and stream pausing**: Selective field masking prevents data leakage while maintaining stream integrity for non-sensitive attributes.
- **Immutable data lakes and end-to-end audibility**: Append-only storage with cryptographic chaining ensures tamper-evident logs for compliance audits.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Edge Policy Engine - consent evaluation stub
function isForwardable(event, policyStore) {
  const policy = policyStore[event.tenantId];
  if (!policy) return false;
  if (policy.consentRevokedAt && policy.consentRevokedAt > event.timestamp) {
    return false;
  }
  return policy.active;
}

// Real-time redaction middleware
function redactPII(event, tenantPolicy) {
  const redacted = { ...event };
  for (const field of tenantPolicy.piiFields || []) {
    redacted[field] = '[REDACTED]';
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model consent revocation state to handle clock skew across distributed edge nodes?
- What consensus mechanism would you use to propagate revocation events across a globally distributed fleet?
- How would you handle in-flight data that was collected before revocation but not yet persisted?
- What metrics would you instrument to verify SLA compliance for the 2-second pause requirement?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to handle real-world edge conditions where connectivity is unreliable, requiring robust reconciliation and canary control across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Edge buffering and eventual reconciliation
- Exactly-once ingestion with sequence numbers
- Per-tenant residency and regional logs
- Deterministic canary rollouts for feature flags
- Testing for outages, clock skew, and data loss

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent ingestion core
class IngestBuffer {
  constructor() { this.buffer = []; this.seen = new Set(); }
  enqueue(event) {
    const key = event.tenantId + ':' + event.seq;
    if (this.seen.has(key)) return;
    this.seen.add(key);
    this.buffer.push(event);
  }
  flush() { /* write to regional store; on failure keep in place for retry */ }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify reconciliation correctness under clock skew?
- How would you scale sequence-number state across millions of tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

Probes OTCA design at scale with multi-tenant isolation, live schema migrations, and regulatory redaction.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA telemetry path design
- Per-tenant residency
- Schema evolution and canary migrations
- Data redaction and immutable logs
- Testing live migrations, rollbacks

## Code Example

```javascript
function redactPII(record, fields = ['user_id','email']) {
  const copy = {...record};
  for (const f of fields) {
    if (f in copy) copy[f] = 'REDACTED';
  }
  return copy;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test canary migrations under multi-region clock skew?
- How would you enforce regulatory redaction across evolving schemas?
